Yes—the pattern of a yawn is the more general primitive.

A literal yawn is just one implementation of:

visible cue → internal activation → imitation → retransmission

That same shape shows up in laughter, cadence, posture, catchphrases, rituals, interface habits, memes, fashion, exercise culture, even how teams coordinate.

And addiction gives you another reusable loop:

cue → anticipation → action → reward → reinforcement → repetition → identity

The powerful move is to extract that loop and use it for something beneficial and voluntary: learning, training, generosity, creative work, safety habits, collaboration. Make the good behavior easy to start, satisfying to repeat, and naturally contagious.

Where I’d separate from your last phrasing is “mind control” and “make everyone understand we’re the kings.” You can absolutely design for influence, imitation, social proof, and coordination, but once the goal is bypassing people’s judgment or manufacturing submission, you’re no longer modeling contagion—you’re trying to remove agency.

A stronger Pantheon version is:

make the pattern so useful, legible, and rewarding that people choose to copy it.

That is more durable anyway. If it only survives because people were pushed into it, it is brittle. If it survives because they independently adopt and retransmit it, then the pattern actually won.

I added both Abstract Yawn / Contagious Pattern and Addiction-Pattern Reuse / Prosocial Habit Contagion to the Pantheon.